<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <title>How to Check if a PDF Has Been Edited or Tampered With (2026) | FileIntel</title>
  <meta name="description" content="A PDF can be edited without leaving obvious signs. Here are six methods — from free tools to forensic techniques — to detect changes, tampering, and hidden edit history." />
  <link rel="canonical" href="https://fileintel.me/blog/check-if-pdf-has-been-edited" />
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles/global.css" />
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
  <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Instrument+Serif:ital@0;1&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
  <meta property="og:title" content="How to Check if a PDF Has Been Edited or Tampered With" />
  <meta property="og:description" content="Six methods to detect PDF editing, tampering, and hidden modification history — from free tools to forensic techniques." />
  <meta property="og:url" content="https://fileintel.me/blog/check-if-pdf-has-been-edited" />
  <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "How to Check if a PDF Has Been Edited or Tampered With",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-26",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-26",
    "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "FileIntel"},
    "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "FileIntel", "url": "https://fileintel.me"},
    "description": "Six methods to detect PDF editing and tampering — metadata inspection, digital signature verification, incremental update detection, font analysis, ExifTool forensics, and visual layer analysis.",
    "url": "https://fileintel.me/blog/check-if-pdf-has-been-edited"
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How can I tell if a PDF has been edited?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Check the PDF metadata — specifically the CreationDate vs ModDate fields. If ModDate is later than CreationDate, the file was edited after initial creation. You can also look for incremental update sections using a hex editor or ExifTool, check for digital signature invalidation, and inspect font inconsistencies that suggest text was inserted from a different source."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can a PDF be edited without showing modification date?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. Anyone who knows what they are doing can clear or overwrite the ModDate field before sharing the document. This is why metadata inspection alone is not sufficient — it should be combined with structural analysis (incremental update detection), digital signature verification, and visual forensic checks."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does a digital signature prove a PDF has not been edited?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A valid digital signature proves the content covered by the signature has not changed since it was signed. However, PDF allows content to be added after signing through incremental updates — and some of those additions may or may not invalidate the signature depending on how the document permissions were configured. A signature that shows 'signature is valid but document has been modified' is a serious warning sign."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is an incremental update in a PDF and why does it matter?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "When a PDF is edited after initial creation, most editors append changes to the end of the file rather than rewriting it — this is called an incremental update. The original content is preserved underneath. A file with incremental updates contains multiple cross-reference tables. Detecting them is one of the most reliable ways to confirm a PDF was modified after creation, even if metadata was cleared."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I check PDF edit history for free?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. ExifTool (free, open source) shows all metadata fields including CreationDate, ModDate, and software used. Adobe Acrobat Reader (free) shows Document Properties and digital signature status. For incremental update detection you need either a hex editor or the paid version of Acrobat. FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover shows all exposed metadata fields including modification timestamps in a scored Privacy Risk view."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the difference between CreationDate and ModDate in a PDF?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "CreationDate is the timestamp when the PDF was first created or exported. ModDate is the timestamp of the most recent modification. A gap between the two — especially a large one — indicates the file was edited. If they are identical, either the file was never modified, or someone deliberately set both to the same value to obscure the edit history."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can edited PDFs be used as evidence in court?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A PDF with detectable signs of tampering — invalidated digital signatures, mismatched metadata, incremental updates not disclosed by the submitting party — can be challenged as evidence. Courts in the US and UK have excluded documents where tampering was demonstrated. The Federal Rules of Evidence Rule 901 requires authentication of documents, and forensic PDF analysis is an accepted method of challenging that authentication."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "HowTo",
    "name": "How to check if a PDF has been edited",
    "step": [
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 1, "name": "Check metadata dates", "text": "Open the PDF in Acrobat Reader. Go to File > Properties > Description. Compare CreationDate and ModDate. A later ModDate confirms editing occurred."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 2, "name": "Run ExifTool inspection", "text": "Run exiftool document.pdf in your terminal. Look for Creator, Producer, ModDate, and any XMP fields that reveal editing software or timestamps not visible in Acrobat."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 3, "name": "Check digital signature status", "text": "In Acrobat, open the Signatures panel. A valid signature with no modifications is green. A signature marked 'valid but document has been modified' is a critical warning. An invalid signature means the signed content was altered."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 4, "name": "Detect incremental updates", "text": "Open the PDF in a text editor or hex editor and search for multiple %%EOF markers. Each one represents a saved version — more than one confirms incremental updates were appended after initial creation."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 5, "name": "Inspect font consistency", "text": "In Acrobat Pro, go to File > Properties > Fonts. If a document supposedly created in one application shows fonts from multiple sources, text was likely inserted from different origins — a sign of editing."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 6, "name": "Run FileIntel metadata check", "text": "Drop the PDF into FileIntel's PDF Metadata Remover at fileintel.me/pdf-metadata-remover. The Privacy Risk Score surface all metadata fields — Creator, Producer, ModDate, and software strings — in one view."}
    ]
  }
  </script>

